problems.problem:
We say that there is an algebraic operation defined on the closed interval $[0,1]$ if there is a rule that assigns to every pair $(a,b)$ of numbers from this interval a new number $c$ from the same interval. We denote it by $c=a\otimes b$. Find all positive $k$ with the property that there exists an algebraic operation defined on $[0,1]$ such that for any $x,y,z$ from $[0,1]$ the following equalities hold:
(i) $x\otimes 1=1\otimes x=x$;
(ii) $x\otimes(y\otimes z)=(x\otimes y)\otimes z$;
(iii) $(zx)\otimes(zy)=z^k(x\otimes y)$.
For all such $k$ define the corresponding algebraic operation.

Let $M$ denote the set of all points with integer coordinates in the rectangular coordinate plane. Prove that there exists a sequence $A_n$ of points of $M$ with the following properties:
1) every point of $M$ is exactly one term of the sequence $A_n$;
2) for any positive integers $i$, $j$ and $k$ whose greatest common divisor is greater than $1$, the points $A_i$, $A_j$ and $A_k$ do not lie on one line.
